                <h2>This Week: Reconciliation Headaches Across Multiple Ad Platforms</h2>
<p>This week, multiple overseas teams reported a familiar frustration at month-end: their ad spend reconciliation doesn't add up. The core issues center on three areas—exchange rate discrepancies in top-ups, unsynchronized spend data, and inconsistent invoice formats. This week's watch breaks down these pain points and offers practical solutions.</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Key Issue This Week</strong>: It's not about whether reconciliation is happening—it's about why the numbers don't match.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>Issue 1: Exchange Rate Gaps Causing Balance Discrepancies</h2>
<p>A utility app team noticed a $200 gap between their expected account balance and actual figures after topping up $50,000 USD. The root cause: overseas ad platforms use "billing rates" or "daily average rates" rather than real-time exchange rates, creating a 0.3%-0.8% variance.</p>
<p><strong>Recommendations:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Record the settlement rate at the time of top-up</li>
<li>Use the platform's "billing rate" for month-end calculations</li>
<li>Create a separate line item for exchange rate differences</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Issue 2: Spend Data Out of Sync Across Platforms</h2>
<p>A cross-border e-commerce team found $250 unaccounted for when comparing platform spend reports. Meta showed $12,350, Google Ads $8,200, and TikTok $3,800—but actual charges totaled $24,100, not $24,350.</p>
<p><strong>Why This Happens:</strong> Data refresh cycles vary. Meta has 2-4 hour delays, Google's "settled amount" lags by 3 days, and TikTok syncs every 24 hours. Using "real-time spend" for reconciliation inflates numbers.</p>
<p><strong>Recommendations:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Use "settled amount" or "actual charge invoices" as your baseline</li>
<li>Establish a T+3 reconciliation cycle for more stable data</li>
<li>Request consolidated statements from your ad agency</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Issue 3: Invoice Formats Differ Across Platforms</h2>
<p>This week's third common issue: invoice headers, tax IDs, and formats vary so much that finance teams reject expense reports. Here's a quick comparison:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Platform</th>
<th>Invoice Cycle</th>
<th>Format</th>
<th>Common Issues</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Meta</td>
<td>Monthly, 5th-10th of next month</td>
<td>PDF + tax slip</td>
<td>Tax rates vary by region</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Google Ads</td>
<td>Monthly + on-demand</td>
<td>PDF</td>
<td>Header may not match agency</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>TikTok</td>
<td>10th-15th of next month</td>
<td>Electronic only</td>
<td>No paper copy in some regions</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Recommendations:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Verify invoice headers match your company's registered name before billing cycles begin</li>
<li>Request "pre-invoices" or "reconciliation proofs" from agencies if early reimbursement is needed</li>
<li>Maintain a multi-platform invoice tracker with billing cycles and expected amounts</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Action Checklist for This Week</h2>
<p>Review these three areas with your operations and finance teams:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Top-up Exchange Rates</strong>: Did you record the settlement rate? Is the rate difference tracked separately?</li>
<li><strong>Spend Data</strong>: Are you using "real-time spend" or "settled amounts"? Did you wait until T+3 to reconcile?</li>
<li><strong>Invoice Status</strong>: Have all platform invoices been issued? Do headers match your company? Any missed prepaid charges?</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h2>Looking Ahead</h2>
<p>Month-end reconciliation is essentially a financial health check for overseas teams. This week's issues highlight the importance of data consistency and standardized processes. Consider establishing a fixed "data alignment day" at the beginning of each month to reduce end-of-month scrambling.</p>
